  "body": "## What was broken\n\nToggling one token-optimizer agent from the tray **silently reverted whatever the dashboard had changed**.\n\n```\n1. dashboard: enable codex        -> disk {claude:F, codex:T, pi:F}\n2. tray:      toggle claude       -> disk {claude:T, codex:F, pi:F}\n                                                    ^^^^^^^ reverted\n```\n\n`_toggle_rtk_agent` flipped one entry in a cache snapshotted at tray construction, then wrote **all three fields** reconstructed from that stale cache.\n\n## Mechanism\n\nThe tray and the admin HTTP API are separate processes that both persist `RtkState` to `~/.fcc/rtk.json`, with no lock and no cross-process notification.\n\nThe API does it correctly — `current = await asyncio.to_thread(load_rtk_state)` before writing. The tray did not. The write itself is atomic (`os.replace`), so this was a **lost update**, not a corrupt file: the setting simply vanished with no error.\n\n## The fix\n\n`_toggle_rtk_agent` re-reads from disk, flips **only** the requested agent on that fresh state, and refreshes the in-memory cache from it so the checkmarks match reality.\n\nNo locking, no IPC, no file watcher — read-modify-write is the whole fix. A comment records *why*, so nobody optimises it back into a cached write later.\n\n### Also fixed — same root cause, smaller form\n\n`Start at Login` and `Tray Enabled` derived their new value from the stale cached field.\n\n`config/desktop.py::_update_state` re-reads before writing, so these could **never clobber a different field** — that part of the original report was wrong and is worth stating plainly. But they could compute the **wrong direction**: if the dashboard turned `start_at_login` on after the tray started, the tray's next click wrote `True` over `True` and *appeared to do nothing*. Both now derive from persisted state.\n\n`_set_server_mode` writes an absolute value and was never affected.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe regression test drives the **real tray menu items**, not the handler in isolation: an external writer enables `codex` after construction, the tray toggles `claude`, and `codex` must survive — plus the cache must be refreshed so the checkmarks are right.\n\nAll three new tests proved real by reverting the fix by hand:\n\n```\nRTK re-read reverted:\n  FAILED test_toggle_survives_concurrent_external_write\n  assert RtkState(claude=True, codex=False, pi=False)\n      == RtkState(claude=True, codex=True,  pi=False)\n  Differing attributes: ['codex']\n\nboolean toggles reverted:\n  FAILED test_start_at_login_toggle_reads_disk_not_the_stale_cache  assert True is False\n  FAILED test_tray_enabled_toggle_reads_disk_not_the_stale_cache    assert False is True\n```\n\nRestored and re-passed afterwards, with no mutation residue left in the file.\n\n```\ntests/cli            285 passed\nfull suite           4424 passed, 14 pre-existing baseline\nruff format / check  clean\nty check             All checks passed!\n```\n\nThe 14 baseline failures are the documented local set (6 PowerShell installer, 8 ambient `EXA_API_KEY`/`SEARXNG`). None touch desktop or tray.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n**The tray still holds an in-memory cache rather than reading state per menu render**, so a checkmark can display a stale value until the next toggle. That is a display concern, not data loss — and the toggle now behaves correctly regardless of what the checkmark showed. Fixing it properly is a menu-rendering change, deliberately out of scope.\n\n**No live desktop run.** `pystray` needs a platform backend, so this is exercised through the menu objects and the state file — the contract the tray shares with the CLI and the dashboard — not by clicking a real tray icon.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti\n",
